 \begin{abstract}
-Federated fine-tuning must reconcile heterogeneous client data with limited training resources. For low-rank adaptation (LoRA), the allocation problem concerns not only adapter capacity but also which target matrices each client should update. This paper presents FedMATRIX-LoRA, a client-aware matrix-level adaptation framework for resource-constrained federated fine-tuning. Each client evaluates candidate matrices using a local mini-batch at the current global adapter and constructs feasible configurations from the resulting importance scores. The server coordinates client participation and matrix configurations under a shared training budget and a round-latency objective. Selected adapters are trained locally and aggregated matrix-wise. This design couples data-dependent adaptation preferences with resource feasibility without changing the LoRA parameterization. Experiments on image classification, language understanding, and dialogue summarization show improvements over the evaluated baselines in task metrics and in resource efficiency under the adopted analytical client profiles.
+Federated fine-tuning must allocate limited training resources across clients with heterogeneous data and device capabilities. For low-rank adaptation (LoRA), this requires deciding not only adapter capacity but also which target projections receive updates. Grouping projections into indivisible Transformer blocks restricts this placement decision. A controlled study at a matched parameter budget finds larger short-term validation-loss reductions with independent matrix selection than with whole-layer allocation in all 12 setting-level comparisons, using either local or server-proxy importance scores. Motivated by this observation, we present FedMATRIX-LoRA, a client-aware matrix-level adaptation framework. Clients estimate update priorities from local gradient profiles and construct resource-feasible matrix configurations; the server coordinates participation and configuration choice under a shared budget and latency objective. Matrix-wise aggregation combines the resulting heterogeneous updates. A conditional stationarity analysis characterizes the roles of update bias and matrix coverage. Experiments spanning image classification, language understanding, and dialogue summarization evaluate the complete framework, while controlled studies examine selection granularity, scoring reliability, and the contributions of individual components. The results support importance-guided matrix selection as a useful adaptation dimension under constrained training budgets.
+
 \end{abstract}
 
