 \section{Conclusion}\label{Sec.conclusion}
-FedMATRIX-LoRA studies client-aware target-matrix selection for resource-constrained federated fine-tuning. Local profiling connects the current adapter to each client's data, while resource-feasible allocation coordinates the resulting preferences across clients. Matrix-wise aggregation supports the resulting heterogeneous update patterns. The reported experiments demonstrate the performance and resource trade-offs of the complete framework, and the conditional analysis relates its average stationarity to effective-update bias and matrix coverage.
+FedMATRIX-LoRA treats individual LoRA target projections as selectable adaptation units under heterogeneous client resources. Its motivating controlled study shows larger short-term gains from matrix-level selection than from whole-layer binding across the tested setting means, using either local or server-proxy scores. The framework evaluates priorities on local data, constructs feasible configurations, and coordinates their execution under a shared budget before matrix-wise aggregation. A conditional analysis relates the resulting selective updates to effective-update bias and matrix coverage. The main benchmarks and controlled ablations assess complementary aspects of this design: the complete framework's performance and resource trade-offs, and the effects of the scoring and allocation mechanisms. Together, these results motivate importance-guided target selection as a practical dimension of resource-constrained federated adaptation.
 
